\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Candidates are assessed on the basis of their fitness, for which they are
 evaluated.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Like other random learning methods, evolutionary algorithms are not affected
 by human bias, but still remain interpretable due to the relatively simple
 models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Genetic Programming
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Comment
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The time-consuming evaluation process can be shortened 2-15 times by using
 Tensorflow and the resulting parallelization on the graphics card 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "karooGPpaper"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Genetic Programming (GP) is an evolutionary machine learning approach that
 automatically generates programs.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The programs are implemented as a programming trees whose genes are its
 nodes.
 The fitness is the reward when evaluated by the RL Environment, for offline
 training on data it is the regression error.
 In genetic programming, both the formula from a set of operators must be
 found as well as the parameterisation of the leaf nodes of a tree, which
 can consist of observations and constants.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The selection process is realised with the help of a tourname selection
 where the best survives from several random candidates of a generation.
 Mutations can affect single nodes as well as complete branches.
 In the case of branch mutations, the complete branch is exchanged and replaced
 with a new, randomly generated branch.
 This can vary in size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In crossover, the branches of two parent trees are exchanged at suitable
 points.
\end_layout

