\begin_layout Paragraph
Pareto GP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The search space of the trees is limited by the tree depth and the number
 of nodes.
 This avoids overvitting, the evaluation of the candidates and the evolution
 process is faster and the number of nodes can be additionally weighted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Instead of storing only the best candidate, a Pareto Front is created, on
 which the fittest candidates for the respective complexity are located.
 This allows the user to select the desired ratio between fitness and complexity.
 For this purpose, the best candidates are put on a Pareto front with the
 fittest candidate depending on their complexity.
 As complexity results from the number of nodes of a tree.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Wrap figure
lines 0
placement o
overhang 0col%
width "50col%"
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ../benchmarks/slurm_runs/MTC200_MSE_scratch/paretofront.pdf
	height 5cm

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GP candidates aligned on the Pareto front.
 With increased complexity the curve usually flattens out.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

Strongly Typed Genetic Programming (STGP) is another extension of GP, where
 an explicit transformation of truth values into numbers and vice versa
 is required 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Strongly_typed_GP"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 In many programming languages this is done automatically, but it is very
 confusing in mathematical expressions.
 A frequent change between data types is not desired anyways.
 Values must be converted explicitly, either by if-statements or comparison
 operators.
 This property can prevent closure in certain constellations, which is why
 this possibility is checked and a mutual exclusive crossover was implemented,
 which always finds two matching nodes in trees.
 Random trees are created by choosing a random depth, up to which the trees
 are built up with random operators.
 A branch can be build in the full and grow mode.
 With the full method, open nodes are always filled up with operators, with
 the grow method terminal nodes are inserted by chance (50%).
 Trees, which were built with the full-method, not only have more nodes
 than trees of the grow-method, but the branching factor is also much bigger
 which leads to an early combination of leaf nodes to complex expressions.
 In grow trees, partial expressions can be considered in isolation for a
 longer time, which can lead to an easier understanding.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:grow-vs-full-method"

\end_inset

 Both approaches can generate important components, so they were used 50%
 each (half ramped mode).
\end_layout

