\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The interpretability is often evaluated by the count of parameters and operators
 and partly additionally by the complexity of interpretation of the operators.
 Also encapsulation of a problem or pattern matching would be properties,
 which could have a general need of properties, which a formula needs to
 have to be explainable, but it would not be accurate enough.
 All these factors are quite good indicators, but they cannot describe the
 effort of a person to understand an approach.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Puzzle example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's consider, for example, the game Puzzle as an NP-hard problem, where
 a whole picture has to be assembled from a pile of pieces.
 Most people know how to recognize colors, that you first have to turn puzzle
 pieces to the colored side and that you should use the picture on the box
 as a reference point.
 A popular tip for beginners is therefore that you should start with the
 edge of the puzzle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is helpful, because the learning process in humans usually consists
 of the composition of many comprehensibly strung together steps.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\end_layout

