\begin_layout Subsection
Tree Edit Distance (TED)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
[xxx Zusammenhang der TED? Erst ]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tree edit distance (TED) is a well-known problem from theoretical computer
 science and will play a crucial role in calculating familiarity.
 It deals with the question, how many elementary changes 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\text{delete},\text{insert,\text{modify}\}}$
\end_inset

 to a tree T are at least needed to create a tree T'.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
“The tree-to-tree correction problem is to determine, for two labeled ordered
 trees and T', the distance from T to T' as measured by the minimum cost
 sequence of edit operations needed to transform T into T'.
 The edit operations investigated allow changing one node of a tree into
 another node, deleting one node from a tree, or inserting a node into a
 tree.” 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "tedOldestPaper"
literal "true"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thanks to numerous improvements made in the last years, the state of the
 art TED computation is robust and fast enough for evaluating numerous of
 GP trees 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "tedAPTED1,tedAPTED2"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 For the experiments the TED was used in its simplest form described above
 due to keeping it simple.
 Nevertheless, there are some conspicuous features and possibilities for
 improvement that should be mentioned, for example the weighted TED or the
 explicit securing of the core structure by fixed nodes.
\end_layout

