\begin_layout Subparagraph
Weaknesses and conceivable improvements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Weighted TED
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The weighting of the nodes is not a trivial task with the TED.
 Therefore the weighted mapping of a tree transformation must be calculated,
 which is not unique and also leaves open questions about how weightings
 should affect the respective changes.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The absolute complexity of the origin is chosen as initial complexity, if
 nodes are removed or changed a part of the weighting is subtracted
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A separate parsimony measurement (possibly as a new dimension of the Pareto
 front)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Explicitly secure the core structure (fixed nodes).
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

As early experiments should show, the TED can lose its protective effect
 if the changes overcome the tree size.
 This is especially true for Origin trees with few nodes or strongly abstracting
 structures (e.g.
 frequent use of if statements).
 In extreme cases, the TED often differs only slightly from node-counting
 distance dimensions.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

When the implicit protection of logical core structures by the TED is not
 sufficient, then logic relevant structures with a very high weight can
 be made robust against changes.
 As extreme form also fixed nodes, which shall be untouched by changes,
 can be defined.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Fixed-nodes"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Preferred Counting of operators
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The structure of a tree is essentially determined by the order of its operators.
 The leaf-node, especially the constants, are in this case with a relative
 high part in the weight of the TED, although the changes are often only
 small adaptations without substantial modification.
 Therefore constants should be much less important.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Verwendung unvollständiger Strukturen als origin (z.
 B.
 fehlende Terminal-Knoten)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Verwendung mehrerer bekannter origins in einem Lauf, wobei beispielsweise
 die kleinste oder mittlere TED verwendet wird.
 So könnten auch mehrere verständliche Ansätze auf ihre Erweiterbarkeit
 direkt verglichen werden oder mehrere Teillösungen separat eingebracht
 werden.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The occurrence of observations could be weighted separately with a relatively
 high value to force a reduced use of them.
\end_layout

