\begin_layout Paragraph
Motivation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The goal of the combined regression error is still to minimise the distance
 to the target solution - only those solutions that require less behavioural
 change should be preferred.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Beispiel 1: Eier Kochzeit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's say we cook eggs for 10 minutes and a target cooking professional
 for 5 minutes.
 When considering whether we should cook our eggs for 3 minutes and 7, 7
 minutes would be preferable as an interim solution, because the extent
 of the changes to our already known cooking behaviour is less.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Überlegungen
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Changes should be made primarily in the direction of the target solution,
 otherwise they are obviously uncertain.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Correct decisions of the starting solution should not be changed.
 Therefore, changes should remain within the corridor around the target
 solution, with the distance to the start solution.
 Punishment should be made in correlation to the size of the corridor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For MDPs: The area where the difference between the start and target solution
 increases can be crucial, as the effect often only becomes apparent later.
 In terms of regression, however, this area is not particularly evaluated.
 In chess, for example, this first deviation would be the indicator for
 a blunder, which could not be corroded even later.
\end_layout

