\begin_layout Standard
In order not to violate the principles of GP, some factors must be considered.
 At the beginning a random number of nodes to be inserted is chosen.
 Instead of the node depth, layers are defined, which are the distance to
 the next fixed node.
 Depending on the number of nodes located on a layer, the number of nodes
 to be inserted is randomly divided into intervals.
 These subtrees are divided according to the Grow or Full method up to the
 interval size they are entitled to.
 The number of nodes must be exactly reached (tolerance -1), otherwise the
 trees could have a large variance of nodes per branch.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When building the branches, a buffer of leaf nodes is built up to avoid
 having to prune the tree at the end.
 To build one level lower, a random node of the ebend is selected and assigned
 according to the Grow or Full method.
 This avoids an overload of the left side, which would occur because only
 limited operators can be used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Allgemeine mathematische Vereinfachung
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Simplifying the mathematical expressions in trees or branches is possible
 if its expression can be written as a less complex version.
 It is a common method to make performant trees more compact without relying
 on a random mutation process.
 For TED simplifications are not trivial to use, because simplified formulas
 are not considered equivalent, for example because of the commutative law.
 
\begin_inset Formula $(a+b)=(b+a)$
\end_inset

 is equivalent, but the TED of the formulas is 2.
 We used Sympy [xx], which is a powerful mathematical tool for python, to
 reduce expressions to the same final form, independent of the start constellati
on.
 
\begin_inset Formula $1+a\longrightarrow a+1$
\end_inset

 Both complete trees and single branches can be simplified, fixed nodes
 have been excluded.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Starting from the basic mathematical form, the branch compiler was used
 to analyze the generated expression and build it into a reduced tree structure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Newly found Pareto entries are always tried to be simplified and saved.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Olymp-reproduction (Pareto reproduction)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similar to the Greek gods who came to earth to reproduce, the pareto candidates
 were also given the opportunity to intermingle with the current population.
 By chance, solutions already found and their genes could be completely
 lost.
 In order not to lose already found solutions permanently, the genes of
 the best candidates are to provide a very small current for backups of
 the best-ofs of the past.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The olymp reproduction includes in particular the possibility for the origin
 tree to reproduce itself.
\end_layout

