\begin_layout Subsection
Divide and conquer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Problems should be broken down into sub-problems as far as possible.
 This reduces the complexity of the problem due to the possible permutations
 of the states.
 For example, if you throw two six-sided dice, there are 
\begin_inset Formula $6\cdot6=36$
\end_inset

 possible permutations.
 If the results are considered in isolation, there are 
\begin_inset Formula $6+6=12$
\end_inset

 possibilities.
 Formulas that are found with genetic programming try to find a formula
 for the whole state space.
 An abstraction of the problem, e.g.
 by if statements, makes the formula grow exponentially larger, which is
 why only rarely if statements are present in formulas found.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
if-operator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In machine learning there are many clustering methods for subdivision; in
 humans, in contrast to if distinctions play an important role.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although it is the most complex function with 3 input parameters, it creates
 more structure than all other operators because its child trees are not
 combined but can be considered separately.
 Nevertheless, the subtrees must have a relation to each other in order
 to harmonize together.
 This combination creates encapsulated programs in a clear way that humans
 can understand more easily.
 It is always to be welcomed when these are contained in programs, but this
 should not be artificially forced, as they are not useful at every point.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Observationen sind nachvollziehbar (Einflussfaktoren)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Observations have a special meaning here, since the only task of the GP
 process is to reduce their values to a result.
 Observations that are not part of the solution are either irrelevant or
 their information can be sufficiently replaced by other observations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The dimensionality of the problem could thus be limited without great risk.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The more isolated the variables are from each other, the less complex their
 interaction is.
\end_layout

